{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 New York Mets were a team defined by wild swings of fortune, a roster that could thrill and infuriate in the same inning, and a season that ultimately fell just short of October baseball. They arrived with high hopes, powered by a fearsome young slugger in Pete Alonso and a bullpen headlined by the expensive offseason acquisition of Edwin Díaz — yet from the very first week, the warning signs were visible. The opener at Washington saw Trea Turner take Justin Wilson deep, and Díaz quickly established himself as one of the most maddening storylines of the year, giving up critical home runs with alarming regularity throughout the first half.\n\nApril ended at .500 and May began to slip, but the Mets refused to fold quietly. A stretch in late May produced some of the season's most electric moments: J.D. Davis lofting a three-run shot, Juan Lagares delivering a clutch double, and the improbable Carlos Gómez launching a walk-off three-run homer that captured everything unpredictable about this club. June, however, brought the year's darkest chapter — a 10-19 collapse that included Seth Lugo surrendering a three-run Javy Báez blast and Díaz again faltering in Philadelphia, leaving the Mets looking more like a .500 team with playoff dreams fading fast.\n\nThen came July, and everything changed. The Mets reeled off a 14-8 record, riding Alonso's historic home run pace, clutch performances from Jeff McNeil, and a suddenly steadying bullpen. August was even better at 17-11, with Todd Frazier delivering timely power and the offense finally living up to its 242-homer potential. The back end of the rotation and relief corps remained shaky — a Dominic Smith error, a late Díaz meltdown on September 3rd that cost a nearly-won game — but the Mets kept grinding.\n\nSeptember saw the Mets play 17-10 ball, teasing a wild card berth that never quite materialized. The season ended on an emotional final day, with Dominic Smith — who had opened the year as a forgotten man — launching a walk-off three-run homer in the finale, a bittersweet exclamation point on a year that promised so much, delivered memorable moments, and ultimately ended 86-76, watching the playoffs from home.",
